\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Inverse components and retained compression}
\date{2026-10-23}

\p{Over a commutative unital ring #{R}, let #{M=X\oplus Y} be a
[chosen splitting](fdip-001T), with ordinary linear
endomorphism #{Q(x,y)=(Ax+By,Ex+Cy)}. Assume that the eliminated
component #{C} is an isomorphism, and put #{S=A-BC^{-1}E}.
Then #{Q} is an isomorphism if and only if #{S} is an isomorphism.
When these conditions hold, all four components of the inverse are
##{\begin{aligned}
\pi_XQ^{-1}\iota_X&=S^{-1}:X\to X,\\
\pi_XQ^{-1}\iota_Y&=-S^{-1}BC^{-1}:Y\to X,\\
\pi_YQ^{-1}\iota_X&=-C^{-1}ES^{-1}:X\to Y,\\
\pi_YQ^{-1}\iota_Y&=C^{-1}+C^{-1}ES^{-1}BC^{-1}:Y\to Y.
\end{aligned}}
In particular, the retained compression of #{Q^{-1}} is #{S^{-1}};
it is not in general #{A^{-1}}.}

\proof{
\p{Use #{Q=UDV} and the explicit inverses of #{U,V} from
[triangular factorization](fdip-001U), where
#{D(x,y)=(Sx,Cy)}. If #{S} is invertible, then
#{D^{-1}(u,v)=(S^{-1}u,C^{-1}v)}. Thus
#{Q^{-1}=V^{-1}D^{-1}U^{-1}} is a two-sided inverse.}

\p{Conversely, suppose #{Q} is invertible. Then
#{D=U^{-1}QV^{-1}} is invertible; write #{J=D^{-1}} and set
#{J_X=\pi_XJ\iota_X:X\to X}. The identities
#{\pi_XD=S\pi_X} and #{D\iota_X=\iota_XS} imply
##{\begin{aligned}
SJ_X&=\pi_XDJ\iota_X=\pi_X\iota_X=I_X,\\
J_XS&=\pi_XJD\iota_X=\pi_X\iota_X=I_X.
\end{aligned}}
Hence #{J_X} is a two-sided inverse of #{S}. This implication still
uses the assumed invertibility of #{C}.}

\p{Applying the three inverse factors to #{(u,v)} gives
##{\begin{aligned}
x&=S^{-1}u-S^{-1}BC^{-1}v,\\
y&=-C^{-1}ES^{-1}u
 +(C^{-1}+C^{-1}ES^{-1}BC^{-1})v.
\end{aligned}}
Setting one input component to zero and projecting the other gives
the four stated map identities.}
}

\p{The real symmetric inverse formula is
\citet{Appendix A.5.5, p. 650}{boyd2004convex}; its first-pivot
convention is exchanged here. The direct proof uses arbitrary unital
#{R}-modules and needs no pairing or symmetry.
Mathlib's \leanref{CategoryTheory.Biprod.isoElim'} constructs
the residual isomorphism when both the first component and the whole
biproduct map are isomorphisms. With summands #{(Y,X)} and component
arguments #{(C,B,E,A)}, it supports the whole-to-residual direction;
it does not by itself assert the four inverse formulas above.}

\p{For a self-adjoint precision with a chosen perfect pairing,
[inverse response](fdip-001Q) supplies its separate response
interpretation. The [cycle calculation](fdip-001Z)
exhibits a retained inverse entry #{2/15} where #{A^{-1}} has entry
zero. No probability law is needed for the map comparison.}
